{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And they shall no more offer their sacrifices to the goat-demons.</b> This pericope was juxtaposed here because there is room for the sectarians to err and say that the dispatch of the goat to the wilderness is to the goat-demons, heaven forbid. Therefore He immediately warned them concerning slaughter outside the Temple precinct, and showed that HaShem desired the opposite — to prevent them from the sacrifice to the goat-demons, which they had formerly practiced, as indicated by the language of עוֹד ('no more'). And the matter of the goat is not worship and sacrifice but rather the portion of Samael. And thus the Ramban explained (Leviticus 16:8), and this is his language: the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded that a goat be sent into the wilderness to the prince who rules over places of desolation, for he is the cause of the stars of the sword and bloodshed and wars and wounds and blows and separation and destruction, and the general principle is that he is a blood-destined soul under the governance of Mars, and his portion among the nations is Esau, who is the nation that inherits the sword and wars; and among the beasts, the goats and the she-goats; and in his portion also the demons called מַזִּיקִין and שְׂעִירִים. And the Rabbi continued at length there in his own words. And now there is room for me to resolve by this that which is stated concerning one who slaughters outside the precinct — 'it shall be considered blood for that man' — which Rashi explained as 'like one who sheds human blood,' etc. And one may ask: what does one who slaughters outside have to do with shedding human blood? Rather, since one who slaughters outside for the goat-demons draws power from Mars, which influences destruction and killing, it turns out that it was well said, 'it shall be considered blood for that man' — for he is indeed considered a shedder of blood, for this is the power of the alien god. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "In the final phrase, 'the power of the alien god' (כֹּחוֹ שֶׁל אֱלֹהוֹ) — retaining the divine epithet as a proper noun per voice rules, though the referent is the force of Samael/Mars, not HaShem."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:06.549501202Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}